        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that differ by a constant factor, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, factor out the integer contents and identify the common primitive polynomial:\n\n$$\n32t^{2}-32t-112=16(2t^{2}-2t-7),\\qquad -20t^{2}+20t+70=(-10)(2t^{2}-2t-7).\n$$\n\nUse the positive contents $16$ and $10$.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(16,10):$\n\n- Multiples of $16:$ $\\,16,\\,32,\\,48,\\,64,\\,80,\\,96,\u2026$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,\\,50,\\,60,\\,70,\\,80,\\,90,\u2026$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(16,10)=80.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((2t^{2}-2t-7),\\,(2t^{2}-2t-7))=(2t^{2}-2t-7).\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(32t^{2}-32t-112,\\,-20t^{2}+20t+70)=80\\cdot (2t^{2}-2t-7)=80(2t^{2}-2t-7).\n$$"
